Full PXE network boot test (DHCP -> TFTP -> NBD -> ldm)
============================================================

What this covers: an end-to-end test of the actual client boot chain a
real thin client goes through - DHCP handing out a boot file, TFTP
serving the bootloader/kernel/initrd, the initrd connecting to an NBD
server for the root filesystem, and switch_root handing off into the
real ltsp6 client system - as opposed to the earlier ldm/xrdp testing
in this repo's other doc files, which ran ldm directly on an
already-booted host and proved the greeter/xfreerdp/xrdp chain works,
but not the network-boot chain itself.

Test setup: an isolated Hyper-V "Internal" switch (no other traffic on
it), a small Debian 13 VM running dnsmasq (DHCP+TFTP in one) and
nbd-server, serving the real client image built earlier (server/doc's
Debian packaging + toram testing) - the actual 638 MiB squashfs image,
containing ldm 3.0.0, X.org and xfreerdp3.


Real bug found and fixed: ldm never actually started at boot
-----------------------------------------------------------------
This is the one concrete, fixed bug this test surfaced, and it applies
regardless of platform (Hyper-V, Proxmox, real hardware): ldm's Debian
package installed and worked fine when invoked directly (as done in
earlier live testing), but had never actually been wired up to start
automatically at boot. It shipped no systemd service unit and never
touched /etc/X11/default-display-manager, so systemd's graphical.target
had nothing to launch a display manager with - boot fell through to a
plain text login prompt instead of the ldm greeter.

Fixed in ldm 3.0.1 (see the ldm-mabutz repository): added
debian/ldm.service, whose "Alias=display-manager.service" line makes
debhelper's dh_installsystemd create the
/etc/systemd/system/display-manager.service symlink that real display
managers rely on, plus postinst/prerm logic to set/clear
/etc/X11/default-display-manager (which ltsp6's own
init-ltsp.d/50-default-display-manager hook already expected to exist).
Verified: after upgrading to 3.0.1 and rebuilding the client image, the
symlink is correctly created on install.


What worked completely: the network boot chain itself
-----------------------------------------------------------
Both of the two standard PXE boot paths were tested and both correctly
completed the full DHCP -> TFTP -> NBD handshake on the first clean
attempt (after fixing an unrelated host-routing issue - see below):

  - BIOS/legacy path (pxelinux): dnsmasq's dhcp-boot serving
    pxelinux.0 directly, a pxelinux.cfg/default with the kernel/initrd
    and a "nbdroot=<server>:<port>/<export>" append line matching
    initramfs-tools' stock nbd script format (see
    /usr/share/initramfs-tools/scripts/local-top/nbd for the exact
    nbdroot= syntax: "server,port" or "server,port/exportname").

  - UEFI path (iPXE, the approach used by the ltsp5-uefi project -
    https://github.com/alkisg/ltsp5-uefi): dnsmasq tags the request
    with dhcp-userclass=set:ipxe,iPXE, serving Debian's prebuilt
    /usr/lib/ipxe/ipxe.efi on the client's first (non-iPXE) DHCP
    request, then a plain iPXE script (kernel/initrd/boot commands,
    same nbdroot= syntax) on iPXE's own second DHCP request once it
    identifies itself. Since this test only needed amd64 (not a 32-bit
    OS on a UEFI client), the simple direct kernel/initrd/boot path
    was enough - no grub chainload workaround needed (that workaround,
    used by ltsp5-uefi, is specifically for booting a 32-bit OS on a
    UEFI client, which iPXE itself can't do).

In both cases, dnsmasq's logs showed the complete expected sequence
(DHCPDISCOVER/OFFER/REQUEST/ACK, each boot file served over TFTP in
order, then a second DHCP round from inside the kernel's own ip=dhcp
network setup) and netstat on the server confirmed a real, established
TCP connection to nbd-server's port - the client was actively reading
its root filesystem over the network, not just failing silently.


Real bug found and fixed: a rogue DHCP-issued default route
-------------------------------------------------------------------
While setting this up, dnsmasq's DHCP handed out a default gateway
option pointing at itself, and the Windows host itself picked up that
lease on its own virtual adapter for the isolated test switch - which
briefly overrode the host's real default route (0.0.0.0/0) with one
pointing into the isolated test network, cutting off ALL internet
access from both the host and WSL2 until noticed and fixed. Two
independent fixes, both worth doing on a real deployment's boot/PXE
server, not just this test: explicitly suppress the router and DNS
DHCP options with empty `dhcp-option=3` / `dhcp-option=6` directives
(clients only need to reach the boot server, not get routed to the
internet through it), and keep DHCP/PXE infrastructure on a switch/VLAN
that nothing else - especially not the management host - has an
interface on.


Known limitation: an unresolved Hyper-V-specific early-boot hang
-----------------------------------------------------------------------
Not fixed, and flagged here honestly rather than papered over: booting
the actual client kernel through this chain, on this Hyper-V host,
reproducibly hangs early in boot - confirmed via frozen network byte
counters (zero new traffic across multiple checks) and a completely
static console (identical screenshots taken minutes apart, down to the
exact same kernel log timestamp).

This was tested on both Hyper-V VM generations, with two independent,
different hang points:

  - Generation 1 (legacy BIOS, pxelinux path): hangs consistently
    around kernel uptime ~55s, right after the "hv_balloon: Max.
    dynamic memory size" log line (Hyper-V's dynamic memory
    ballooning driver - present and logging even with the VM
    configured for static memory, so its presence alone isn't
    inherently the cause). Blacklisting the hv_balloon module via a
    "module_blacklist=hv_balloon" kernel parameter made things worse
    (no console output at all), so that's not the fix either, and was
    reverted.

  - Generation 2 (UEFI, iPXE path): hangs much earlier, around kernel
    uptime ~0.25s, partway through core security-subsystem
    initialization (IMA/EVM/AppArmor logging), well before any
    Hyper-V-specific or network/storage driver would even be reached.

The same kernel and the same client image boot and run completely
normally under nested Proxmox VE/QEMU (see the Proxmox HA testing
elsewhere in this repo - four separate VMs on that stack all boot this
kernel fine) and when ldm is run directly on a already-booted host (see
the ldm/xrdp end-to-end testing). That rules out the kernel or the
client image itself as the cause and points at something specific to
how this particular Hyper-V host boots a guest through this exact
chain.

Three targeted fixes were tried on the Generation 2 case, based on
what the boot log showed right before each freeze - none resolved it:

  - The log's last line before freezing was consistently right around
    IMA/EVM loading X.509 certificates from the UEFI "db" certificate
    store ("integrity: Loading X.509 certificate: UEFI:db"). Added
    "efi=noruntime" to stop the kernel from making EFI runtime calls
    at all. This did visibly change the log (the UEFI:db certificate
    lines disappeared entirely, confirming the parameter took effect)
    and let boot reach a little further in one run - but did not fix
    the hang, which still occurred a little later at the same kind of
    point.
  - One run's log showed "scsi_host0" right before freezing, on a VM
    that has no disks attached at all (network boot only) - a known
    class of hv_storvsc issue when a SCSI controller has zero disks.
    Removed the VM's SCSI controller entirely (Remove-VMScsiController
    - Generation 2 VMs otherwise keep an empty one by default even
    with -NoVHD). This didn't help either, and the next run actually
    froze slightly *earlier* than before.
  - The freeze point itself isn't fully deterministic - it's moved
    between roughly kernel uptime 0.18s and 0.25s across different
    boots of the identical configuration, all still well within the
    same early security/IMA/EVM initialization phase. That variability
    is itself informative: it points more toward a timing-sensitive
    race in VMBus channel setup than a single deterministic blocking
    call, which is a harder class of bug to pin down without
    lower-level tracing (kernel debug builds, Hyper-V-side VMBus
    tracing) than was practical in this session.

Given all of this, further Hyper-V-specific debugging was set aside
here rather than pursued indefinitely - the PXE/TFTP/NBD chain and the
ldm packaging fix are the solid, real results of this test; the
early-boot hang is a genuine, reproducible, but currently unresolved
Hyper-V compatibility issue best picked up again with more specialized
tooling.
